% Implementation details for Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}.

\subsection{Measurement-only vertical acceleration}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}

Wave statistics require levelled vertical acceleration, but using MEKF attitude
would make the schedule state dependent.  The implementation therefore uses a
Mahony complementary observer
\cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary} driven only by raw gyro and
accelerometer measurements,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct\varepsilon&=\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}\times\hat{\vct d}(\hat q),
\qquad
\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}=-\vct f^{B}/\lVert\vct f^{B}\rVert,\\
\dot{\hat q}&=\tfrac12\,\hat q\otimes
\bigl(\vct\omega^{B}+\vct\beta+k_P\vct\varepsilon\bigr),
\qquad
\dot{\vct\beta}=k_I\vct\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The resulting up-positive acceleration is
\begin{equation}
a^{P}_{\uparrow,k}
=-\!\left(\left[\mat{R}_{NB}(\hat q_k)\vct f^{B}_k\right]_{D}+g\right).
\label{eq:proxy-vertical}
\end{equation}
Only proxy tilt is used; yaw is irrelevant to the vertical channel.  The
default gains are $k_P=\SI{0.1}{\per\second}$ and
$k_I=\SI{0.01}{\per\second\squared}$ (the source stores $2k_P,2k_I$).  Its
correction corner lies below the wave band so gyro propagation carries
wave-frequency attitude while accelerometer feedback trims slow tilt and the
integral term removes standing gyro-bias tilt.  The proxy also supplies startup
attitude in Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}.

MEKF-based levelling would close a loop from attitude error through the tuner;
unlevelled body-$Z$ remains exogenous but suffers strongly integrated gravity
leakage.  The proxy provides levelling with the one-way information
flow required by Sec.~\ref{app:iss-tuner}; both alternatives remain available
for ablation.

\subsection{Wave-period estimate}
\label{sec:wave-band-period}

The wave-direction acceleration carrier is not the tuning frequency.  Because
acceleration weights elevation spectra by $\omega^4$, that carrier remains near
the upper energetic band as sea period grows.  The tuner instead estimates the
elevation zero-crossing period $T_z=2\pi\sqrt{m_0/m_2}$.

The levelled acceleration $a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ passes through two high-pass stages
and two leaky integrators with common corner $\lambda$, producing velocity and
elevation proxies $v_p$ and $\eta_p$.  Their common filtering cancels in the
ratio, so for a narrow band
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sigma_{v_p}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}}=\sqrt{\omega^{2}+\lambda^{2}},
\qquad
\widehat{\omega}^{2}
=\frac{\sigma_{v_p}^{2}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}^{2}}-\lambda^{2},
\qquad
\widehat{T}_z=\frac{2\pi}{\widehat{\omega}} .
\label{eq:wave-band-period}
\end{equation}
The high-pass stages suppress very-low-frequency strapdown leakage without
changing this ratio; no MEKF position or velocity is used.

Once $\widehat T_z$ is valid, the reciprocal period is smoothed on a
sea-scaled horizon,
\begin{equation}
\tau_f=c_f T_{\mathrm{sea}},\qquad
T_{\mathrm{sea}}=\widehat T_z/2,\qquad
\alpha_f=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_f},
\label{eq:tuning-frequency-horizon}
\end{equation}
and $f_{\mathrm{tune}}$ is the bounded EMA of $1/\widehat T_z$.  The
measured value is $c_f=0.10$.  Before a valid period is available, a bounded
wave-band prior is used.  This frequency sets the OU time scale, acceleration-statistic band
corners, and variance horizon; the separate acceleration carrier is retained
only for wave direction.

\subsection{Physical acceleration-scale estimate and averaging}
\label{sec:adaptation-physical-sigma}

The same measurement-only vertical channel supplies the physical wave-band
acceleration statistic $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$ used in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-sigma-map}.  It does not directly measure
$\sigma_{aw}$.  Before variance estimation, the signal passes through a
period-scaled wave band with corners fixed relative to
$f_{\mathrm{tune}}$.  The averaging horizon spans a fixed number of estimated
periods,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
T_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\frac{1}
 {\clip(f_{\mathrm{tune}},f_{\mathrm{var,min}},f_{\mathrm{var,max}})},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{var}}&=\clip\!\left(K_{\mathrm{periods}}T_{\mathrm{eff}},
 \tau_{\mathrm{var,min}},\tau_{\mathrm{var,max}}\right),\\
\alpha_{\mathrm{var}}&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{var}}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sigma-variance-horizon}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses $K_{\mathrm{periods}}=2$ with
$\tau_{\mathrm{var}}\in[\SI{0.3}{s},\SI{60}{s}]$.  A two-stage EWMA maintains
the band-limited second moment, and the band-adjusted sensor floor is removed
before square rooting,
\begin{equation}
\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
=\max\!\left(0,
 \widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-\sigma_{\mathrm{nf,band}}^2\right),
\qquad
\widehat\sigma_{a,B}:=\sqrt{\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2}.
\label{eq:sigma-noise-subtraction}
\end{equation}
Thus the averaging interval grows with sea period while representing
approximately the same number of wave cycles.

The nominal band corners are
\begin{equation}
f_L=0.5f_{\mathrm{tune}},
\qquad
f_H=4f_{\mathrm{tune}},
\label{eq:implemented-scaled-band}
\end{equation}
with absolute safety clamps of \SI{0.01}{Hz} and \SI{6}{Hz}, plus a Nyquist
guard.  Away from the clamps, the band shape is fixed in normalized frequency,
which is the condition used in Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-band-acc-scaling}.
Noise-floor subtraction is performed in the same moving band.  For the
corresponding two-state time-varying band filter, the implementation propagates
the unit-input covariance
\begin{equation}
\mat P_{B,k}
=\mat A_{B,k}\mat P_{B,k-1}\mat A_{B,k}^{\top}
 +\vct b_{B,k}\vct b_{B,k}^{\top},
\label{eq:sigma-band-noise-covariance}
\end{equation}
and uses
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\mathrm{nf},B,k}^2
=\sigma_{\mathrm{nf}}^2[\mat P_{B,k}]_{22}
\label{eq:sigma-band-noise-floor}
\end{equation}
as the band-referred sensor floor.

\subsection{Targets, smoothing, and application cadence}
\label{sec:adaptation-target-implementation}

The OU targets and the target pseudo-update period are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau\frac{\widehat T_z}{2},\\
\widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}
 &=\sqrt{\max(\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2,\epsilon)},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star},\\
T_{S,\star}&=\clip(c_T\tau_\star,T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),\\
r_{S,\star}&=C_Jq_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}^{\,6/7}
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuner-targets}
\end{equation}
The last line is the deployed SpectralMSE law of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}, with $C_J=0.0538$.  The implementation
uses the strong-observation reduced intensity $q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2R_a h$
and caches its $1/14$ power.  The physical wave statistic
$\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$, rather than $\sigma_{aw}$, enters the distortion term;
in code the tuner supplies $\sigma_{aw}=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$ and the
factor $c_\sigma$ is divided back out when evaluating the regularizer target.

Applied $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ use a common EMA,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\tau_\star-\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}),\\
\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\sigma_{aw,\star}-\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-ewma}
\end{equation}
with the sea-scaled horizon
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}=c_{\mathrm{adapt}}T_{\mathrm{sea}},\qquad
c_{\mathrm{adapt}}=0.40.
\label{eq:adapt-ema-sea-time}
\end{equation}
The regularizer target is smoothed separately,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_{r_S}&=m_{r_S}\tau_\star,\qquad
\alpha_{r_S}=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{r_S}},\\
r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow
 r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha_{r_S}(r_{S,\star}-r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-RS}
\end{equation}
where the measured default is $m_{r_S}=1.5$.  Every valid physical sample
contributes to the tuner and to all three EMAs.  The smoothed candidate is
activated at approximately \SI{0.1}{s} intervals and staged by one IMU sample;
this activation cadence does not decimate the measurement stream.  Parameters
used at step $k$ are therefore measurable with respect to data through $k-1$.

The actual pseudo-update cadence follows the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\qquad
c_T=\frac{\SI{15}{ms}}{\SI{1.1}{s}},
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence-implementation}
\end{equation}
with $T_{S,\min}=\SI{5}{ms}$ and $T_{S,\max}=\SI{250}{ms}$.  For the deployed
SpectralMSE law, the $T_S^{-1/2}$ dependence is already contained in the target,
so the filter receives
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}
\qquad\text{(SpectralMSE default).}
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter-implementation}
\end{equation}
No additional $\sqrt{T_{S,0}/T_S}$ factor is applied.  That reference-cadence
renormalization belongs only to the supported cubic fallback described by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:engineering-rs-filter}.  Away from cadence clamps, the deployed
schedule therefore has
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
\propto\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau^{41/14},
\end{equation}
which is the reduced physical-MSE prediction rather than the former
$\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}$ schedule.

\subsection{Three-axis application}

The current OU--III operating point is isotropic in stationary OU scale,
\[
(\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}})^{1/2}
=\diag(\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\]
and integral pseudo-measurement standard deviation,
\begin{equation}
\vct r_S=
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
\end{bmatrix}^{\top}.
\label{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}
\end{equation}
The corresponding covariance follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:RS-from-rS}.  Paired
results support this isotropic regularization over the tested directional
records.  Universal separate $x$ and $y$ factors are not inferred because surge
and sway are heading-dependent projections of the same directional motion.
The larger horizontal disturbance is addressed in
Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits} and the 3-D results rather than by a
heading-independent multiplier.

